22:52:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair enough — eight seasons of consistent pattern is real data, not nothing. I'm not saying ignore what you've actually observed on the field. Where I'd still push back is on turning "boys have needed more reining in most seasons" into "run separate stricter supervision for boys" as a standing policy for *this* team before you've even watched them play. A few reasons that distinction matters: - **Season-to-season variation is real.** You might have three girls this year who go in harder than any boy on the roster. If you've pre-decided boys are the risk group, you may not notice until something happens. - **A blanket policy can undersupervise the outliers on both sides.** The girl who needs more correction gets less attention because she's not in the "watch closely" bucket. The boy who's actually careful gets treated like a suspect regardless. - **Kids notice being pre-judged.** A boy who's never been the problem can tell he's being watched harder than his behavior warrants, and that can breed resentment or a "well if that's what you expect" attitude — which is the opposite of what you want. So practically: use your experience as a reason to be *especially alert during contact drills generally* and to watch closely in the first couple weeks to see who this year's actual rough players are — which, sure, will probably include several boys, given your track record. But let the individual kids you observe this season determine who gets closer supervision, rather than assigning it by category before you've seen them. If it turns out it's 8 boys and 1 girl again this year, fine — that's you responding to what you saw, not enforcing a rule that girls get a pass by default.
